A||N||6||2FAST4U||Can anybody answer clutch question ?||||5||
Z||000000||2FAST4U||06-22-2000||09:09 PM||||The Iron Duke uses an 8 1/2" clutch and the 2.8 uses a 9 1/8" clutch when used in the Fiero but when used in the Camaro/Firebird the Duke uses the same 9 1/8" unit as the 2.8 does. Anybody know of any reason the 9 1/8" clutch wouldn't fit the Fiero if I had the Flywheel drilled for it? They both have the same input shaft.<BR>Any comments would be apreciated.||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||06-23-2000||12:32 AM||||I hate to answer a question with more questions, but why do you want to know?  <BR>What are you planning to do?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>I mean, are the 8-1/2" replacement clutches unavailable?  Think you get more torque capability with the larger clutch, but the smaller clutch is probably perfectly adequate for the L4 (no offense, Iron Duke fans!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ).  And if you plan to change to a bigger engine, it usually comes with its own flywheel.<P>Just curious - ! <P>   ||||reg||5
Z||000002||2FAST4U||06-23-2000||12:44 AM||||Because - 165 HP plus 50 HP NOs = 215 HP and it may just be me but I bet the stock 8 1/2" disk will have a short life span.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieroshop||06-23-2000||12:49 AM||||Well let's see. Big is not always better. We have proven that with by comparing the larger clutch to the smaller disk and have found out that the smaller disk fabricated right will outperform and outlast the larger disk. Check it out at <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.net" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroshop.net</A> <BR>scroll down and you will see a picture of the clutch.||||reg||1
Z||000004||2FAST4U||06-23-2000||02:56 AM||||OK, I agree that bigger is not always better, but I have a couple of questions for you then.<P>1.Higher clamping force means higher spring rates. How does the throw out bearing, release lever and shaft bearings hold up under the additional load.<P>2. Since I'm having an aluminum flywheel made anyways and since I am considering your disk or a Center Force dual friction unit why wouldn't your 9 1/4" unit be even better than your 8 1/2" since there is no additional cost to have the flywheel drilled for it. If it will work.<P>Thanks for the response.<P>Joe||||reg||1
Z||000005||GT Bastard||06-24-2000||08:31 PM||||A friend of mine is running the larger disk in his 2M4's, and it works just fine.||||reg||1
Z||000006||SeanyD||06-25-2000||02:44 PM||||I just need a little clarity here, I have an 85 2.8L that's pretty much reached the stage of parts car. and a nice red 84 2.5L. When we picked up the 85, the guy had just put a brand new clutch in it, is it possible for me to take the new clutch from the 85 and put it in the 84?  because the clutch in my 84 went last winter!!  any help would be great!!||||reg||5
